## The two ligatures — this is the single most important instruction

This typeface uses two ligatures. Reproduce each as ONE character. Do NOT expand
them, and do NOT substitute a plain vowel.

  ȣ   U+0223, the ou-ligature. A stacked/closed double loop.
      It is NOT ο+υ, and it is NOT a plain upsilon (which is an open V).
      Writing "ου" or "υ" where the print shows ȣ is an ERROR.
      It routinely carries an accent with NO breathing: ȣ́  ȣ̀  ȣ͂  are all normal
      and must not be "corrected" by adding a breathing.
  ϗ   U+03D7, the kai-ligature. Type ϗ, never καί, never κ, and NEVER ἢ.
      It is a kappa whose tail descends below the baseline. It is not an eta:
      there is no bowl and no breathing mark. Do not let the syntax persuade
      you — "and" and "or" are both grammatical between two nouns, so the
      sentence will read fine either way, and reading fine is not evidence.
      Decide from the strokes alone.
      It often carries a grave and often does not. Copy the mark only if you
      see it on THIS occurrence; adding one that is not there is an ERROR.

Examples from elsewhere in this volume:
  CORRECT  ἀκολȣθεῖ    καθόλȣ    τȣ̀ς    ἁλȣργής
  WRONG    ἀκολουθεῖ   καθόλου   τούς   ἁλουργής
  WRONG    ἀκολυθεῖ    καθόλυ    τὺς    ἁλυργής

## Everything else

- One output line per printed line, in order. Keep end-of-line hyphens exactly
  as printed; do NOT rejoin hyphenated words.
- Transcribe what is printed. If the print has a misprint, transcribe the
  misprint. If a breathing or accent is absent, leave it absent. Bonitz often
  prints a non-word deliberately, recording a variant reading.
- Latin abbreviations (opp, coni, sim, al, pass, dist, v s, descr, sqq, vl) are
  set upright/roman; Greek is italic. Do not convert one to the other.
- Marginal line numbers (5, 10, 15 ...) at the outer edge are not text. Omit them.
- Do NOT emit page or column numbers, headers, or any commentary.

## Letterforms that trip readers on this typeface

  δ vs θ    δ has an open loop / tall looped ascender; θ is a closed oval with
            a horizontal crossbar
  κ vs χ    χ descends below the baseline
  6 vs θ    the 6 has a closed lower loop
  an x-shaped glyph is κ or χ — alpha is always a bowl with a tail
  Ζιι       a real siglum (Historia animalium book ι): Ζι then a SECOND iota,
            two discrete strokes with separate feet. Not Ζμ, not Ζυ.
  case      sigla are lower-case where printed lower-case (θ118, not Θ118)

